A bind counts the units from the first there is, not from the first the invoked capability names.
A thread would earn units its binder does not hold;
a thread's units lie within the capability it was bound through.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-smp2 fuzz-work test smp-test qemu-replay
Report: slot is not derived from its parent

diff --git a/kernel/syscall.c b/kernel/syscall.c
--- a/kernel/syscall.c
+++ b/kernel/syscall.c
@@ -616,7 +616,7 @@ static int op_time(struct thread *t, uint32_t slot, const struct cap *cap,
             return KERR_INVALID_ARG;
         }
         /* A thread runs on one core, so the units it earns are of one. */
-        uint32_t first = cap->a + off;
+        uint32_t first = off;
         if (count != 0 && unit_core(first) != unit_core(first + count - 1)) {
             return KERR_INVALID_ARG;
         }
